- What are the trade-offs vs using a compiler intrinsic?

## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit SWAR techniques and per-byte manipulation, not simply whole-value reversal.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR bit-reversal
- Mask design across 16 bytes
- Branch-free parallel bit-twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint128 M1 = ...; // 0x5555... across 128 bits
  const uint128 M2 = ...; // 0x3333...
  const uint128 M4 = ...; // 0x0F0F...
  // stage 1: swap adj bits
  x = (x >> 1) & M1 | (x & M1) << 1;
  // stage 2: swap bit-pairs
  x = (x >> 2) & M2 | (x & M2) << 2;
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x >> 4) & M4 | (x & M4) << 4;
  // stage 4: reverse within bytes
  // (use an appropriate mask like M8)
  return x;
}
```

