## Why This Is Asked
Reversing 128 bits tests a candidate's ability to decompose 128-bit data into two 64-bit lanes and apply a branch-free bit-reversal at the lower level.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value represented as {hi, lo}
- constant-time 64-bit bit-reversal using masks
- composing to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(v){
  v = ((v >>> 1) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((v & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  v = ((v >>> 2) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((v & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  v = ((v >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((v & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  v = ((v >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  v = ((v >>> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  v = (v >>> 32) | (v << 32);
  return v;
}
function reverseBits128(x){
  // x.hi, x.lo are 64-bit values represented as BigInt
  return { hi: reverse64(x.lo), lo: reverse64(x.hi) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type if provided?
- What are the trade-offs of using 64-bit reversals vs a single 128-bit table lookup on platforms with broad SIMD support?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise understanding of bit-rotation, modulo-64 behavior, and edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Rotation via (x << s) | (x >> (64 - s)) with s = n & 63
- Safe when s = 0 using masking (64 - s) & 63
- No loops or conditionals; only shifts and ORs

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript BigInt version for 64-bit rotation
function rotl64(x, n) {
  const s = BigInt(n & 63);
  const MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  return ((x << s) | (x >> ((64n - s) & 63n))) & MASK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit types?
- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to implement a branchless, constant-time parity on 128-bit data, a common requirement in cryptography and error-detection code. It tests knowledge of SWAR reductions and handling of multi-word integers.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parity reduction
- 128-bit representation via two 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling without branches
- Correctness over both halves

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hi = x.hi, lo = x.lo;
  hi ^= hi >> 32; hi ^= hi >> 16; hi ^= hi >> 8; hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  lo ^= lo >> 32; lo ^= lo >> 16; lo ^= lo >> 8; lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  return (unsigned)((hi ^ lo) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity128 to count parity modulo 4?
- What are performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

- Demonstrates ability to replace branches with bit-twiddling for performance.
- Applies a well-known, portable technique to a common memory-allocation problem.
- Handles edge cases and overflow without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix-fill trick to propagate the highest set bit.
- Branchless arithmetic to compute next power of two.
- Overflow behavior on 32-bit boundaries.

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t nextPowerOfTwo32(uint32_t n) {
  // Assumes n > 0. No explicit branches.
  n--;
  n |= n >> 1;
  n |= n >> 2;
  n |= n >> 4;
  n |= n >> 8;
  n |= n >> 16;
  return n + 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this behave for n = 0x80000000 or 0xFFFFFFFF?
- Can you adapt this to 64-bit inputs with no loops?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses proficiency in low-level bit manipulation using only shifts and masks—skills essential for systems programming, embedded development, and cryptographic code. It demonstrates understanding of parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR/SIMD Within A Register) techniques.

## Key Concepts
- **Bit masking and shifting:** Extracting and repositioning specific bit ranges
- **Parallel processing:** Operating on multiple data elements (bytes) simultaneously via mask patterns
- **Byte-local vs. global reversal:** This approach reverses bits within each byte independently, unlike global bit-reversal algorithms
- **Magic constants:** The hexadecimal masks (`0xF0F0F0F0`, `0xCCCCCCCC`, `0xAAAAAAAA`) are standard patterns for these swaps

## Complete Code Example
```c
uint32 reverseBitsInEachByte32(uint32 x) {
  // Step 1: Swap adjacent nibbles (0xF0 = 11110000 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xF0F0F0F0) >> 4) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);

  // Step 2: Swap adjacent 2-bit pairs (0xCC = 11001100 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xCCCCCCCC) >> 2) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);

  // Step 3: Swap adjacent individual bits (0xAA = 10101010 in each byte)
  x = ((x & 0xAAAAAAAA) >> 1) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);

  return x;
}
```
